# V. Risk factors

#5.1 New games do not show the expected risks (high)
- The failure of the 2026 core focus on the Sea of Forgotten and Infinity will have a direct impact on growth expectations
- The game industry is essentially a "explosive driver," and no single game can be successful.
- Old IPs are stable but limited in growth space, and new IPs have to be continuously connected.

#5.2 Game industry regulatory risk (middle-high)
- China ' s policy of restricting play by minors may be further tightened
- The uncertainty of the rhythm of the release and approval could affect the online schedule of the new game
- The content review policy may limit the development space for certain types of games

##5.3 Risk of overconcentration of operations (middle-high)
- 80% + income dependent on a single business for games, lacking a multi-purpose hedge
- Small and limited growth of non-play business (docture, cloud music, innovation)
- There is little buffer for companies if the overall game industry is exposed to systemic risks (e.g., policy tightening)

## 5.4 Competition increases risk (medium)
- The structural advantages of telecommunications in social distribution channels and IP reserves persist
- New studios such as Mikhail have performed strongly in terms of globalization and access for young users
- AI lowers the threshold for game production, which may lead to more competitors entering

##5.5 Critical person risk (medium)
- Ding Ding, as founder and CEO, has a decisive influence on the strategic direction of the company
- Ding-Ting's personal preferences (such as the cautious attitude to the AI model "not blindly pursuing the Universal Model") may miss the opportunity.
- The company lacks a clear succession plan.

