# Six, investment arguments

# 6.1 Look at the core logic

1. ** Game business is a high-quality cash flow machine**: Māori is close to 70%, ROE is stable at 22-24%, and operating cash flow is about $40 billion a year. Twenty-three years of Dreams is still at a new height in DAU, and this long-lived IP compound is extremely rare in the global game industry. The "recurring income" attributes of play business are systematically underestimated in the market.

2. **The valuation has a significant margin of security**: about 16 times the PE is below the historical average by 20; about $19.8 billion in net cash represents 25 per cent of the market value, after which only 12 times the net profit is deducted. Even without considering the up-front space of the new game line, the current price provides better security cushions based on the cash flow capacity of the current operation.

**The new game pipe in 2026 could become a catalyst** The Sea of Forgotten Loss projected to be Q3 (with expected first year running at $2.55 billion) and the Infinite Big was recognized by Morgan Chase as a potential product of the highest income-scale income-prone scale in Internet history. Any product that meets expectations will significantly enhance market confidence in the growth prospects of the network.

# 6.2 Look out core logic

1. ** Overdependence on old IPs and uncertainty about success rates for new products**: Web-friendly growth patterns are highly dependent on a few long-lived IPs, and the relay of new products has not yet been proven. There are also cases in history where too many new games have failed to perform as expected. The story of "Old Tree Flowers" cannot be perpetuated forever.

2. ** Business homogeneity limits valuation ceiling**: 80% + income from games and non-playing business never reaches scale. Compared to the diversity of business (play + social + financial technology + cloud), Internet-friendly valuations should naturally be lower than integrated Internet companies. The market may have given 16 times PEs as a reasonable price rather than as an undervalued one.

3. **The Chinese play industry faces structural constraints**: the protection of minors policy limits the growth of the user base, uncertainty about the number approval increases the risk of new products being introduced, and AI may lower barriers to entry into the industry and increase competition. The web-friendly "high Maori plus low growth" model may further tilt towards "low growth".

